---
import Layout from '@layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import staticSvg from '../../assets/static_gradient.svg'
import animatedSvg from '../../assets/animated_gradient_css.svg'

const envSvg = import.meta.env.DEV ? staticSvg.src : animatedSvg.src
---

<style>
  .teal-svg {
    background-position: center center;
    background-size: cover;
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
  }
</style>

<Layout title="Public Service Application UX | Mike Earley">
  <main
    class="teal-svg relative z-10 min-h-screen pb-16 shadow-xl shadow-darkTeal-600"
    style={`background-color: #0b2228; background-image: url(${envSvg})`}
  >
    <section class="mx-auto flex max-w-5xl flex-col gap-12 px-6 pb-10 pt-12 text-lightTurquoise-500">
      <header class="flex flex-col gap-4">
        <h1 class="font-display text-4xl md:text-5xl">
          Public Service Application UX
        </h1>
        <p class="max-w-2xl text-base font-semibold tracking-wide md:text-lg">
          Design exploration of a high-stakes public service enrollment flow,
          focused on clarity, accessibility, and applicant confidence.
        </p>
        <ul class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 text-xs font-semibold uppercase">
          <li class="rounded-full bg-darkTeal-600/70 px-3 py-1">
            Public-facing
          </li>
          <li class="rounded-full bg-darkTeal-600/70 px-3 py-1">
            Compliance-driven
          </li>
          <li class="rounded-full bg-darkTeal-600/70 px-3 py-1">
            Mobile-first
          </li>
        </ul>
      </header>

      <section aria-labelledby="public-problem" class="flex flex-col gap-4">
        <h2 id="public-problem" class="font-display text-2xl">The problem</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-lightTurquoise-600">
          Applicants navigating eligibility-based public service enrollment
          workflows often struggle to understand progress, required
          documentation, and submission status—particularly when requirements
          are conditional and compliance language is presented without clear
          guidance.
        </p>
        <figure class="rounded-2xl border border-darkTeal-300/60 bg-darkTeal-800/40 p-6">
          <!-- TODO: Replace with problem overview image -->
          <div class="aspect-[16/9] w-full rounded-xl bg-darkTeal-900/60"></div>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="public-persona" class="flex flex-col gap-4">
        <h2 id="public-persona" class="font-display text-2xl">
          Understanding the applicant
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-lightTurquoise-600">
          This work focused on applicants completing long, interruption-prone
          public service applications primarily on mobile devices.
        </p>
        <figure class="rounded-2xl border border-darkTeal-300/60 bg-darkTeal-800/40 p-6">
          <img
            src="../../assets/danielle_persona_full.png"
            alt="Primary public service applicant persona overview."
            class="w-full rounded-xl"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption class="mt-3 text-xs text-lightTurquoise-600">
            Primary persona used to evaluate clarity, progress visibility, and
            document requirements.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="public-service-blueprint" class="flex flex-col gap-4">
        <h2 id="public-service-blueprint" class="font-display text-2xl">
          Service blueprint
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-lightTurquoise-600">
          To identify where applicants lose confidence or abandon the process,
          the end-to-end enrollment flow was mapped across user intent,
          front-stage UI, and back-stage system behavior.
        </p>
        <figure class="rounded-2xl border border-darkTeal-300/60 bg-darkTeal-800/40 p-6">
          <img
            src="../../assets/Blueprint_v4.png"
            alt="Service blueprint mapping applicant enrollment flow."
            class="w-full rounded-xl"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption class="mt-3 text-xs text-lightTurquoise-600">
            Key UX risk points occur when requirements are conditional, work
            happens offline, and application status is unclear.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section
        aria-labelledby="public-progress-models"
        class="flex flex-col gap-4"
      >
        <h2 id="public-progress-models" class="font-display text-2xl">
          Exploring progress models
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-lightTurquoise-600">
          We explored multiple progress and requirement models to help
          applicants understand what was required, what had been completed, and
          what remained—without relying on late-stage error messages.
        </p>
        <div class="grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-3">
          <figure class="rounded-2xl border border-darkTeal-300/60 bg-darkTeal-800/40 p-4">
            <!-- TODO: Replace with progress model concept A -->
            <div class="aspect-[4/3] w-full rounded-xl bg-darkTeal-900/60"></div>
          </figure>
          <figure class="rounded-2xl border border-darkTeal-300/60 bg-darkTeal-800/40 p-4">
            <!-- TODO: Replace with progress model concept B -->
            <div class="aspect-[4/3] w-full rounded-xl bg-darkTeal-900/60"></div>
          </figure>
          <figure class="rounded-2xl border border-darkTeal-300/60 bg-darkTeal-800/40 p-4">
            <!-- TODO: Replace with progress model concept C -->
            <div class="aspect-[4/3] w-full rounded-xl bg-darkTeal-900/60"></div>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="public-direction" class="flex flex-col gap-4">
        <h2 id="public-direction" class="font-display text-2xl">
          Final direction
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-lightTurquoise-600">
          A guided checklist-based enrollment model that made requirements
          explicit, surfaced document needs early, and separated plain-language
          guidance from compliance detail.
        </p>
        <figure class="rounded-2xl border border-darkTeal-300/60 bg-darkTeal-800/40 p-6">
          <!-- TODO: Replace with final direction image -->
          <div class="aspect-[16/9] w-full rounded-xl bg-darkTeal-900/60"></div>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="public-results" class="flex flex-col gap-4">
        <h2 id="public-results" class="font-display text-2xl">
          Resulting design
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-lightTurquoise-600">
          The final experience clearly communicates progress, outstanding
          requirements, and application status across mobile and desktop
          contexts, supporting both assistive navigation and applicant
          confidence.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-4 md:flex-row">
          <figure class="flex-1 rounded-2xl border border-darkTeal-300/60 bg-darkTeal-800/40 p-6">
            <!-- TODO: Replace with desktop design image -->
            <div class="aspect-[16/10] w-full rounded-xl bg-darkTeal-900/60"></div>
          </figure>
          <figure class="md:w-1/3 rounded-2xl border border-darkTeal-300/60 bg-darkTeal-800/40 p-6">
            <!-- TODO: Replace with mobile design image -->
            <div class="aspect-[9/16] w-full rounded-xl bg-darkTeal-900/60"></div>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </section>
      <p class="text-xs text-lightTurquoise-600">
        Note: This case study represents an independent UX design exploration
        informed by real-world public service workflows. It is not affiliated
        with or endorsed by any government agency.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>
